docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:15:4. D's framing 'authorship in body, custody in `sig`' (brief:54-55) is wrong as stated for facts and batches. Verified: arrival.py:1338/1345 calls `signer(observer, content_commitment(...))`, and libs/custody/src/custody/signing.py:103-150 (`fact_signer_for`) resolves `keys/<observer>/ed25519.key` — the AUTHOR's per-observer key, selected BY the envelope observer value. The outer arrival signature on a fact is today signed by the author's own key, not the custodian's. `sig` is not a custody claim on this path; the envelope observer is load-bearing for key selection, not a decorative echo.
docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:33:**Forward costs**: THE LABEL SUB-CHOICE IS NOT ONE OPTION, IT IS THREE, and it must be pinned in the same ruling: (a) the tick's own name — today's ruled-out convention; (b) the log's own genesis observer via `self._log.genesis()['observer']` inside ArrivalStore._write — zero new plumbing, memoized, the most literal reading of 'consistent with genesis', but merge.py's `_entry_for` has no live ArrivalLog in scope and would have to open the source log a second time to get it; (c) a live vertex/operator identity — the brief's 'the vertex's own observer, e.g. project' — which is NOT reachable today: ArrivalStore.__init__ takes no custodian kwarg, open_canonical_store forwards only signer callables (compiler.py:956-962), and tick_signer_for (signing.py:75-88) returns a bare digest->sig closure carrying no identity string. (b) is free; (c) is the only one matching the brief's phrasing and it is not free. Ticks-outer-signed rider: A's claim is UNVERIFIABLE while ticks stay outer-unsigned — `_resolve` is gated on `_SIG in record` (arrival.py:1746) and every tick mint passes signer=None, so a tick's envelope observer is never looked up in the registry. A raises the signing question without needing it answered; the ruling should defer it on purpose. rows[0] wart: INHERITED (A is tick-scoped). Dual-signature principle: A leaves the fact/batch outer sig exactly as it is (author's key over author's content), so it neither confirms nor contradicts the principle — it makes the tick record honest without touching the seam. Git-diff readability: preserved, the 'who' column stays populated for every kind.
docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:51:**Blast**: ~6 code sites + 3 doc sites + ~11 test files. Code: arrival_store.py:462 (fact AND tick both change), :526 (batch — the row read disappears entirely), arrival.py:813/822 (genesis — already custody, unchanged), arrival.py:1329-1346 / 1400-1417 (the `signer(observer, content_commitment(...))` coupling — the load-bearing one), and any future production key mint (none exists). Grammar: NO shape change — arrival.py:390-391 stays one uniform non-empty rule, which is exactly what makes C the 'one consistent meaning' option. Vector regen: ZERO (body.observer is untouched, so the whole fold/lens/merge/replay/witness corpus is unaffected). Test blast lands entirely on the Python arrival suite — 11 files construct records with explicit `observer=` for fact/batch/key kinds (test_arrival_authority, _key_registry, _store, _grammar, _gate, _authority_gate, _cas_head, _derived_log, _projection, _rederivation_rowids, _coordinate_d0). Docs: wire-format.html:159 needs no edit, but the seams callout (240-254) currently scopes the change to ticks and would actively contradict a C ruling, and the Fact worked example (369-392, envelope `"observer":"kyle"` at :378 mirroring body `"observer":"kyle"` at :385) models exactly the echo C kills and must be regenerated to show divergence.
docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:53:**Forward costs**: C's premise is contradicted by the implemented machinery, and this is the single most important verified fact in the table. `fact_signer_for` (signing.py:103-150) resolves `keys/<observer>/ed25519.key` — the author's per-observer key — selected by the envelope observer value passed at arrival.py:1338. Today a fact's outer arrival signature IS an authorship signature, signed with the author's key and verified against that observer's registry entries (`_keys_valid_at(registry, record['observer'], ordinal)`, arrival.py:1783). Under C every fact/batch outer sig is instead produced by the custodian's key, uniformly. Per-author attestation does not vanish — it survives in the inner fact-domain row signature, carried verbatim through merge — but the outer signature stops being an authorship claim for facts and the per-observer key machinery built by design/fact-signing-per-observer-keys is exercised by exactly one identity for every record kind. That is a substantive narrowing of what `verify_authorship` establishes and must be stated as an accepted consequence in the ruling, not discovered later. rows[0] wart: KILLED outright — the field stops reading any row. Dual-signature principle: C makes the outer sig cleanly custodial and the inner sig cleanly authorial, which is the tidiest reading of wire-format's 'two subjects (author content, custody record)' — but see the doc conflict in rider notes; it is the reading protocol.html §06 currently denies. Git-diff readability: preserved for facts/batches (body.observer still in the line) and improved in honesty.
docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:55:**Risks**: The signer-selection decision must be pinned explicitly: either (a) choose a custody label that already resolves a key — the self-observer `vertex_path.stem` works today via the flat-layout delta-2 fallback (signing.py:127,141-142), or (b) decouple signer-selection identity from the envelope field value, which the brief does not surface at all. Multi-observer key introductions stop being exercised by any current record kind. And the wire-format seams callout will read as false the day C ships unless rewritten in the same change.
docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:61:**Blast**: Largest by far: ~13 code sites across 2 libs + 6 doc sites + ~15-20 test files. Code: RECORD_FIELDS (arrival.py:111), `_validate` check (:390-391), `content_commitment` signature and envelope dict (:537-551), `build_record` assembly (:603), the `Entry` dataclass field (:705, which merge.py constructs), `_commitment_of` (:1808-1813), the append_marked/append_marked_many build closures (:1338-1346, :1400-1413), `_walk_authority` genesis seeding (:1733,1737), `_resolve` (:1745-1805), plus both mint call sites (arrival_store.py:462, :526) and merge.py:749-763. Vector regen: ZERO checked-in JSON, but ~15-20 Python test files embed `observer=` kwargs or exact commitment digests — these are behavior tests, so 'regen' means updating call sites and inline record literals, not regenerating fixtures. Docs: the heaviest doc blast of any candidate — wire-format.html:159, :171 (live-file key order), :195-196/247-248 (this is 'dropped', not 're-spelled' — rewrite, not re-word), :199-212 (genesis/key body profiles need to say where the founder identity now lives), :303 (the commitment formula), :357/378/385 (three example literals); index.html:351,360; protocol.html:216, :347-360 (including the commitment equation at :355); backend-contract.html:180,482.
docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:63:**Forward costs**: D AS LITERALLY SCOPED IS NOT SELF-SUFFICIENT. For genesis and key introductions the envelope observer is the ONLY persisted identity: arrival.py:1733 seeds `registry[record['observer']]` from it, and `_resolve` (:1783) uses it for every signed record. The genesis body is ratified as exactly {protocol, lineage, key} (decision:design/arrival-sliceA-authority §2.3, pinned by test_genesis_body_is_the_ratified_three_fields). Drop the field with no body replacement and the founding key has no identity to be valid FOR — key_registry cannot seed, verify_authorship cannot resolve, and no fact signed under the founding key verifies. Fact/batch could re-source from body.observer for free (it is an echo); genesis and key-introduction cannot, so D requires reopening a ratified body shape — a second amendment the brief's cost accounting (commitment shape + lost diff column) does not mention. Second forward cost: the `observer=` PARAMETER survives on mint/append/append_marked even after D, because it still selects the signer and still seeds the registry — D shrinks the wire record without shrinking the call-site API, so a reviewer grepping `observer=` will see it survive nearly everywhere. rows[0] wart: NOT killed — arrival_store.py:526 still picks which observer's key signs the batch; D only makes the choice invisible. Git-diff readability: worse than the brief states, and for a precise reason — `.arrival` is one JSON object per line with no pretty-printing, so a diff hunk is always a whole-line addition and hunk granularity is not the cost. The cost is eyeball scannability of a raw tail: fact and batch lines still carry body.observer, so D costs nothing there, but a genesis body is {protocol,lineage,key} and a tick body is {name,payload} — for those two kinds the envelope observer is the only 'who' anywhere in the line, so D does not move the who-column, it deletes it. Dual-signature principle: D collapses the outer sig to a bare custody mark, which is the strongest form of the wire-format reading and the strongest contradiction of protocol.html §06.
docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:65:**Risks**: Two commitment-shaped functions are easy to confuse and must not be: engine.arrival.content_commitment(k, at, observer, origin, body) is D's target; engine.sqlite_store's row-level `_fact_commitment_hash(kind, ts, observer, origin, payload)` is the legacy inner commitment (consumed at merge.py:584 and sqlite_store.py:1797) and MUST keep its observer arg. Same arity, same string types, different subject. Beyond that, the registry-seeding gap is blocker-grade for D's 'maximal dissolution' framing: as scoped it does not dissolve, it defers the problem into a ratified body schema.
docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:73:The verified constraints funnel toward A by elimination, not by enthusiasm. C's premise — 'the outer signature is a custody claim, so the envelope observer should name the custodian' — is contradicted by the implemented machinery: `fact_signer_for` resolves the AUTHOR's per-observer key by the envelope observer value, and `_resolve` verifies against that author's registry entries, so today's outer fact signature is an authorship signature. C is therefore not a relabel; it re-keys every fact and batch outer signature from author to custodian and narrows what verify_authorship establishes. That may still be the right call, but it is a different and much larger decision than 'pick a spelling for the tick observer', and it should not be made as a side effect of closing a tick seam. D as literally scoped is not self-sufficient: dropping the field leaves genesis and key introductions with no persisted identity to seed the key registry, and the genesis body is a ratified three-field shape, so D requires reopening a ratified fact — it defers the problem rather than dissolving it, while carrying the largest doc and test blast of the four. B is the cheapest-looking option but costs a kind-conditional branch in a validator that is deliberately kind-agnostic (the `_placement_fault` split is the evidence), and — the decisive point — it forecloses outer-signed ticks, because the custody signer explicitly refuses an empty observer by design. Choosing B is choosing to close the second agenda item, not defer it. A is the least-cost coherent option: no grammar change, no commitment-shape change, no re-keying of any signature, zero vector regen, and it leaves both the fact/batch semantics and the tick-signing question open for a later ruling made on its own merits. Its honest weakness is that A's claim is unverifiable while ticks are outer-unsigned — but an unverifiable true claim is strictly better than the verifiably false one on the wire today ('a name is not an authorship claim', already ruled), and the deferral is recoverable in a way that C's re-keying and D's body amendment are not. Everything here is free only until the first `.arrival` store exists; A is also the option that leaves the most doors open after that point passes.
docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:79:RIDER 2 — should ticks gain outer signatures? Three verified facts bear on the deferral. (a) It is currently a clean non-question: `_resolve` is gated on `_SIG in record` (arrival.py:1746), every tick mint passes signer=None (arrival_store.py:465), and merge's Entry has no signer field at all — deliberately, per its docstring ('a signer here would be a parameter with no caller and an invitation to fabricate authorship'). So no tick's envelope observer is ever looked up in the key registry today, under any candidate. (b) B forecloses the option: signing.py's `_keypair` guard refuses an empty observer by design, so an empty-observer tick cannot be outer-signed without reworking custody. A and C leave the door open; D closes it differently (nothing left to key a signer selection on for ticks). (c) A makes the deferral visible rather than silent, which is a virtue: A puts a claim on the wire that is true but unverified, and the ruling should say so in those words. Panel position: defer tick signing explicitly, and record that wire v1 pins with ticks outer-unsigned ON PURPOSE — the brief already asks for this and the evidence supports granting it.
docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice1-panel-synthesis.md:81:RIDER 3 (unasked, but load-bearing and the panel flags it) — THE TWO DOCS CONFLICT ON WHAT THE OUTER SIGNATURE IS, and one of them will be false whichever candidate wins. protocol.html §06 (:347-355): 'Arrival separates what an observer authored from where a custodian placed it. The optional record signature covers the content commitment... A signed fact can therefore move between custody lineages without fabricating a new author signature' — that is the outer sig as the AUTHOR's portable signature. wire-format.html's ruled-seams callout (:240-254): 'The inner fact-domain signature beside the outer arrival-content signature KEEPS — two subjects (author content, custody record)' — that is the outer sig as the CUSTODY record. The code splits the difference and both halves are verified: it is signed by the author's per-observer key (protocol.html's half — signing.py:103-150, arrival.py:1338) but minted only in the home lineage and dropped at merge, since Entry carries no signer and merged records ride with only the inner row signature carried verbatim (wire-format's half, and the task framing's 'per-lineage'). The session cannot pick a meaning for the envelope observer without implicitly picking a side here, because that field is what selects the signing key. Recommend the ruling state which half is law and mark the losing prose for rewrite in the same change — A and B leave the conflict standing (fine, if named); C and D each resolve it toward custody and make protocol.html §06 false as written.
